# Trials of Atlantis and Catacombs as rumours only (owner, 2026-11-15, "Yes agreed on lore"). Nobody in this world has been
# to a sunken land or under a capital, nobody has master levels, artifacts or a Catacombs class, and no line suggests the
# player can go. Atlantis is sailors' and grandmothers' talk; what lies under the capitals is a door that stays shut.
#
# The tag: @topic rumour_atlantis or rumour_below. Only lines with that topic may say "atlantis" or "catacombs"
# (tools/bot-voices/build_corpus.py, ERA_RUMOUR_TOPICS); every other out-of-era word stays banned in them too.
# Where they are heard (rare by design):
#   - the storyteller's legend of the glass that spoke (topic tale_atlantis, AutonomousTales.Legends), in the tale rotation;
#   - one /faction debate (an ordinary scene, one of several hundred);
#   - a few place-bound /faction musings (AutonomousChatFiller: musings are a 5% share of /faction openers, one written
#     line per realm in 20 minutes);
#   - answers when a player asks ("what is atlantis", "is there anything under camelot", LoreLegends).
# New writing; Mythic's lore (camelotherald.com/node/244) paraphrased, never quoted.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the storyteller's legend: the glass that spoke
# Rewritten 2026-11-15 after the owner's read ("choppy, the vocab is poor"). The teller writes whole sentences with
# capitals and stops, which the chat pass keeps (AutonomousChatDebateSpeech: it drops only a line's last stop and sends a
# line of more than 14 words as two chat lines, at a sentence or the comma nearest its middle). The listeners type as
# players do.
@kind debate
@realm *
@topic tale_atlantis
@levels 1-50
@scene tale_atlantis
@role A type=*
@role B type=*
@role C type=Socialite,Explorer
@role D type=*
@role E type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role F type=Leveler,Crafter,Explorer
A: {name_b} you on? tell the one about the glass that spoke | {name_b}, do your gran's story, the glass ball one
C: oh, this one. hush everyone | quiet in faction for a bit, {name_b} is telling the orb
?E: the talking ball again lol | here we go, the ball that talks
D: go on {name_b}, the new ones have never heard it | please {name_b}, i never heard it all the way through
B: This one is my family's. My grandmother's grandmother saw it, when she was a girl in service at the council hall.
B: One hard winter a fisherman came up from the coast with a sack over his shoulder, and the sack was still dripping.
B: His nets had come up heavy off the point, and in them was a ball of green glass the size of a child's head.
B: Coral had grown over one side of it, and it was warm to the touch, though it had lain all night in the bilge.
B: The council paid him two silver and set it on the high table between the candlesticks, and for seven nights it was a curiosity.
~ 8
D: and then it talked? | so when does it talk?
B: On the eighth night, late, with the hall dark but for the high table, a light came up in the heart of the glass.
B: In the light stood a man no taller than her thumb, robed in a fashion no tailor in the realm had ever seen.
B: He spoke in a stranger's voice, and he said that past the edge of the sea there lay a land where whole peoples were tried.
B: He asked the council to send its champions over the water, and he named the ten trials one after another.
?F: did they send anyone? | so who went?
B: Nobody went. They argued until the candles guttered, and then the light came up again and he said it all over, word for word.
B: She was the one who saw that he never looked at the councillors. He looked down the hall, to where nobody sat.
B: He said it every night that winter. The council stopped coming, and in the end only she heard it, because the candles still wanted trimming.
B: For years after, she dreamed of it: sand to the end of the world, a mountain on fire, a city standing on cloud.
B: And at the last the sky opened and the sea stood up, and all of it was gone in a day and a night.
B: In spring they sewed it back into the fisherman's sack and rowed it out past the point on the ebb.
B: She was in the boat, because someone had to hold the sack. She said it was still talking when it went under.
~ 25
F: ...still talking | wait. it was still talking?
A: ty {name_b}. not going near the docks tonight | thanks {name_b}, nobody tells that one like you
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- a /faction debate: the uncle's city
@kind debate
@realm *
@topic rumour_atlantis
@levels 1-50
@scene rumour_atlantis_uncle
@role A type=Socialite,Explorer
@role B type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role C type=*
@role D type=*
A: {q} my uncle says theres a whole city at the bottom of the sea lol
B: your uncle also says he soloed the dragon
A: ok he said that once. but the fishermen really do pull up carved stone with coral all over it
C: the sailors have a name for it, atlantis | harbour folk call it atlantis and then go quiet
?D: so what are the lights out past the harbour at night? | then what are the lights under the water
B: fish that glow. or your uncle with a lantern
A: laugh all you like. ask any fisherman if he will cast a net past the point after dark
B: your uncle would. right after the dragon
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- answers: "what is atlantis?" (LoreLegends)
@kind reply
@topic rumour_atlantis
@answers ask_atlantis
@realm alb
@about place
[gothwaite harbour talk|sailor talk from caer gothwaite]. a land past the isles that sank in a day and a night, and nobody has been
@realm mid
aegirhamn sailor talk, a drowned land out past the isles. buy one an ale and you hear it twice
@realm hib
the boatmen at the domnann dock [talk about it|swear by it], a sunken land out west. none of them has rowed out to look

